[{:seon.task/name "the-chats-order-is-the-order-its-entities-were-made"
  :seon.task/plan "The model's side of pages-from-data step 2 (Sean, 2026-11-10: \"How do we know what order messages come in? Sort them by time?\"; ruled with Point: the time is the transaction that made each entity, no new clock attribute, no read mark). What exists: seon.ctx.wire/turn-entities places a turn's messages by seon.ctx/placed-messages, which derives which turn read a message from its delivery against the model calls' bases, and orders evals by their call (seon.ctx/turn-evals). The change: one function of the pulled data gives the chat's sequence, and both the chat's hiccup (Web's walk) and the native messages walk it: each chat entity (a model call, an eval, a message) in the order the transactions that made it ran, read from the pull with no db read per entity (on an agent's branch every chat entity is made there, so its entity id is its creation order: show it before relying on it); a model's eval sorts with its call, so a reply's tool results stay right after its tool-call message (a provider refuses a user message between them); a message sits at the first call made after it, so a message delivered while a turn's evals ran reads at the next turn, after them. placed-messages and turn-evals as page logic go. Example sessions (seon.ctx.session/session-tx, example-rekey) write their records one transaction each, so the same order holds for them. Cost: a seek per window of the chat, never a sort inside a render. test/seon/recorded_requests.edn changes once, named in the commit that changes the order (or not at all, if the order is the same)."
